# consul/self-registration

**Alternative starter.** The recommended way to register services is `consul/service-discovery`, where a Consul sidecar agent registers each service and the service contains no Consul code. Use this one to see what that sidecar saves you from: here the service registers itself with the [Consul API](https://developer.hashicorp.com/consul/api-docs/agent/service).

- `cmd/self-greeter`: an HTTPS service on port 8443. On start it registers itself, with a TLS health check, under the name `self-greeter`. On SIGTERM it deregisters itself.
- `cmd/self-caller`: every 2 seconds, asks Consul for healthy `self-greeter` instances and calls one of them.

Breadboard sets `CONSUL_HTTP_ADDR`, `CONSUL_CACERT`, `BB_FQDN`, `TLS_CERT_FILE`, `TLS_KEY_FILE` and `SSL_CERT_FILE`. The code reads nothing else.

```sh
bb up
bb logs self-caller -f
```

What the service now owns, compared with the sidecar:

- **Registration code** in every service, in every language you use.
- **Cleanup.** Deregistering on shutdown is the service's job. A crash skips it, so the registration sets `DeregisterCriticalServiceAfter` as a safety net.
- **Signals.** The service must receive SIGTERM to clean up, so it has to be the container's main process. Breadboard starters compile in the image and run the binary directly, which is what makes this work.
- **Which agent.** With no local agent, the service registers with the Consul server, so its entry lives on the server's node rather than its own.

## Try this

- **Watch the registration.** `bb logs self-greeter` shows `registered`; `docker stop bb-{{.Experiment}}-self-greeter-1` shows `deregistered`.
- **Scale out.** Copy `self-greeter` to `self-greeter-2` in `experiment.yaml` and run `bb up`. Each registers under its own ID with the same name, and the caller sees 2 healthy instances.
- **Compare.** Apply `consul/service-discovery` to the same experiment and compare `cmd/greeter` with `cmd/self-greeter`.

## Break this

- **Crash instead of stopping.** `docker kill -s KILL bb-{{.Experiment}}-self-greeter-1`. No deregistration runs: the entry stays, its check fails, and Consul removes it after a minute.
- **Skip the signal.** Set the `command` to `[go, run, ./cmd/self-greeter]` and stop the container: `go run` does not pass SIGTERM on, so `deregistered` never prints.
- **Register a wrong address.** Register `localhost` instead of `BB_FQDN`. The check fails and callers that do get the address cannot verify its certificate.
